      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mott%2C_North_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Some say the town was named after Lillian Mott, secretary to the promoter; others credit a different namesake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mott%2C_North_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An art‑deco arched 'rainbow' bridge once spanned the Cannonball River, linking Mott to West Mott.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mott%2C_North_Dakota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1903, the William Brown Land Company of Chicago plotted a town on the Cannonball River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discovermott.com/our-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Cannonball Trail, a mile and a half long, winds along the riverbanks for hikers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.discovermott.com/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mott holds the quirky title of being the smallest town worldwide to appear on classroom and library globes.",
